\h Adapa
\toc1 Adapa
\mt1 Adapa
\c 1
\cp 1
\p
\v 1 \vp 1\vp* He possessed intelligence ..........
\v 2 \vp 2\vp* His command like the command of Anu ..........
\v 3 \vp 3\vp* Wide intelligence he (Ea) made perfect for him, the destiny of the country to reveal.
\v 4 \vp 4\vp* Unto him wisdom he gave; eternal life he did not grant him.
\v 5 \vp 5\vp* In those days, in those years the wise man of Eridu,—
\v 6 \vp 6\vp* Ea as a chief (?) among men had created him,—
\v 7 \vp 7\vp* A wise man whose command no one could restrain,
\v 8 \vp 8\vp* The prudent, the most wise among the Anunnaki was he,
\v 9 \vp 9\vp* Blameless, clean of hands, anointed, the observer of divine commands,
\v 10 \vp 10\vp* With the bakers he made bread,
\v 11 \vp 11\vp* With the bakers of Eridu he made bread,
\v 12 \vp 12\vp* The food and water of Eridu he prepared daily,
\v 13 \vp 13\vp* With his clean hands he prepared the table,
\v 14 \vp 14\vp* And without him the table was not cleared.
\v 15 \vp 15\vp* The ship he steered; fishing and hunting for Eridu he did.
\v 16 \vp 16\vp* Then Adapa of Eridu,
\v 17 \vp 17\vp* While Ea lay upon a bed in a chamber (?),
\v 18 \vp 18\vp* Daily the closing of Eridu he made right.
\v 19 \vp 19\vp* At the pure quay, the quay of the new-moon, he embarked upon the ship,
\v 20 \vp 20\vp* The wind blew, his ship sailed,
\v 21 \vp 21\vp* With the rudder he steered the ship
\v 22 \vp 22\vp* Upon the broad sea.
\q1 ........................................................
\c 2
\cp 2
\p
\v 1 \vp 1\vp* ....................................
\v 2 \vp 2\vp* The south wind [blew and capsized him],
\v 3 \vp 3\vp* To the house [of the fishes] it made him sink,
\v 4 \vp 4\vp* “O south wind [increase] thy rage as much as [thou art able],
\v 5 \vp 5\vp* Thy wing I will break.” As he spoke with his mouth,
\v 6 \vp 6\vp* The wing of the south wind was broken, seven days
\v 7 \vp 7\vp* The south wind blew not on the land. Anu
\v 8 \vp 8\vp* To his messenger, Ilabrat, said:
\v 9 \vp 9\vp* “Why has the south wind not blown upon the land for seven days?”
\v 10 \vp 10\vp* His messenger Ilabrat answered him, “My lord
\v 11 \vp 11\vp* Adapa, the son of Ea, the wing of the south wind
\v 12 \vp 12\vp* Has broken.” Anu, when he heard this,
\v 13 \vp 13\vp* Cried “Help!” He ascended his throne: “Let some one bring him to me.
\v 14 \vp 14\vp* Likewise Ea, who knows the heavens, summon him,
\v 15 \vp 14a\vp* To King Ea to come.”
\v 16 \vp 14b\vp* To him he caused word to be borne,
\v 17 \vp 14c\vp* .......... To him, to King Ea,
\v 18 \vp 14d\vp* He sent a messenger.
\v 19 \vp 14e\vp* He is of great understanding, he knows the hearts of the great gods,
\v 20 \vp 14f\vp* .......... of the heavens, he establishes it.
\v 21 \vp 15\vp* [A soiled garment he made] him wear; with a mourning garment clad him,
\v 22 \vp 16\vp* He clothed him and gave him counsel,
\v 23 \vp 17\vp* Saying: “Adapa, into the presence of Anu, the king, thou art going,
\v 24 \vp 18\vp* Fail not the order, my word keep,
\v 25 \vp 19\vp* When thou goest up to heaven and approachest the gate of Anu,
\v 26 \vp 20\vp* At the gate of Anu, Tammuz and Gishzida
\v 27 \vp 21\vp* Stand, they will see thee, they will ask: ‘Lord,
\v 28 \vp 22\vp* For whose sake art thou thus, Adapa? For whom
\v 29 \vp 23\vp* Art thou clad in a mourning garment?’ ‘In our country two gods have vanished, therefore
\v 30 \vp 24\vp* Am I thus.’ ‘Who are the two gods who in the land
\v 31 \vp 25\vp* Have vanished?’ ‘Tammuz and Gishzida.’ They will look at one another and
\v 32 \vp 26\vp* Be astonished. Favorable words
\v 33 \vp 27\vp* To Anu they will speak. A joyful countenance of Anu
\v 34 \vp 28\vp* They will reveal to thee. When thou standest in the presence of Anu,
\v 35 \vp 29\vp* Food of death they will offer thee to eat;
\v 36 \vp 30\vp* Thou shalt not eat. Water of death they will offer thee to drink;
\v 37 \vp 31\vp* Thou shalt not drink. A garment will they show thee;
\v 38 \vp 32\vp* Put it on. Oil they will set before thee; anoint thyself.
\v 39 \vp 33\vp* The command which I give thee, forget not. The word
\v 40 \vp 34\vp* Which I have spoken hold fast.” The messenger
\v 41 \vp 35\vp* Of Anu came: “Adapa of the south wind
\v 42 \vp 36\vp* The wing has broken. Into my presence bring him.”
\v 43 \vp 37\vp* The road to heaven he made him take and to heaven he ascended.
\v 44 \vp 38\vp* When to heaven he ascended, when he approached the gate of Anu,
\v 45 \vp 39\vp* At the gate of Anu, Tammuz and Gishzida were standing.
\v 46 \vp 40\vp* When they saw him they cried: “Adapa, help!
\v 47 \vp 41\vp* Lord, for whose sake art thou thus?
\v 48 \vp 42\vp* For whom art thou clad in a mourning garment?
\v 49 \vp 43\vp* In the country two gods have vanished; therefore in a mourning garment
\v 50 \vp 44\vp* Am I clad. Who are the two gods who from the land have vanished?”
\v 51 \vp 45\vp* “Tammuz and Gishzida.” They looked at one another and
\v 52 \vp 46\vp* Were astonished. When Adapa before Anu the king,
\v 53 \vp 47\vp* Approached, Anu saw him and cried:
\v 54 \vp 48\vp* “Come, Adapa, why of the south wind the wing
\v 55 \vp 49\vp* Hast thou broken?” Adapa answered: “Anu, my lord,
\v 56 \vp 50\vp* For the house of my lord in the midst of the sea
\v 57 \vp 51\vp* I was catching fish. As I was midway of the voyage
\v 58 \vp 52\vp* The south wind blew and capsized me;
\v 59 \vp 53\vp* To the house of the fishes it made me sink. In the anger of my heart
\v 60 \vp 54\vp* [The south wind] I cursed. At my side answered Tammuz
\v 61 \vp 55\vp* And Gishzida: ‘The heart should be toward Anu.’
\v 62 \vp 56\vp* They spoke, he was appeased, his heart was won (?).
\v 63 \vp 57\vp* “Why has Ea, to impure man, of the heavens
\v 64 \vp 58\vp* And the earth revealed the heart?
\v 65 \vp 59\vp* Strong (?) has he made him (Adapa); a name he has given him.
\v 66 \vp 60\vp* We—what can we do to him? Food of life
\v 67 \vp 61\vp* Bring him, that he may eat.” Food of life
\v 68 \vp 62\vp* They brought him; he ate it not. Water of life
\v 69 \vp 63\vp* They brought him; he drank it not. A garment
\v 70 \vp 64\vp* They brought him; he clothed himself. Oil
\v 71 \vp 65\vp* They brought him; he anointed himself.
\v 72 \vp 66\vp* Anu looked at him; he wondered (?) at him.
\v 73 \vp 67\vp* “Come, Adapa, why dost thou not eat nor drink?
\v 74 \vp 68\vp* Now thou shalt not live; men are mortal (?).” “Ea my lord
\v 75 \vp 69\vp* Said: Thou shalt not eat, thou shalt not drink.”
\v 76 \vp 70\vp* Take him and bring him back to earth.
\v 77 \vp 71\vp* ............ looked upon him.
\c 3
\cp 3
\p
\v 1 \vp 1\vp* ........................
\v 2 \vp 2\vp* He commanded him and he ...........
\v 3 \vp 3\vp* The garment, he commanded him and he clothed himself.
\v 4 \vp 4\vp* ...... Anu wondered greatly at the deed of Ea.
\v 5 \vp 5\vp* The gods of heaven and earth, as many as there are: “Who is thus mighty (?)?
\v 6 \vp 6\vp* His command is the command of Anu. Who can surpass [him]?”
\v 7 \vp 7\vp* As now Adapa from the horizon to the zenith of the heavens
\v 8 \vp 8\vp* ...... looked, he saw his terror ...... (i. e., the terror he inspired)
\v 9 \vp 9\vp* [Which] Anu concerning Adapa upon him ...... had placed.
\v 10 \vp 10\vp* [The service (?)] of Ea he made his satisfaction.
\v 11 \vp 11\vp* Anu fixed as his lot his lordship in brilliance to the distant future.
\v 12 \vp 12\vp* .......... Adapa, the seed of mankind,
\v 13 \vp 13\vp* [Who] victoriously broke the wing of the south wind,
\v 14 \vp 14\vp* And to heaven he ascended. “Thus let it be!”
\v 15 \vp 15\vp* ...... that which he in evil ways imposed on the people,
\v 16 \vp 16\vp* .......... sickness which he placed in the bodies of people.
\v 17 \vp 17\vp* ........ Ninkarrak appeased.
\v 18 \vp 18\vp* Sickness [shall co]me, his disease be violent,
\v 19 \vp 19\vp* .......... destruction shall fall upon him,
\v 20 \vp 20\vp* [In] good sleep he shall not rest,
\v 21 \vp 21\vp* .... shall overturn (?) the joy of people’s hearts.
